          Updated October 6, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#belt-top-surface" class="btn-ghost">Clean the Belt</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          A treadmill collects sweat on the top surfaces and dust everywhere else. Neither is a
          dramatic problem on a given day, but sweat is mildly corrosive and dust ends up under the
          belt and in the motor's cooling air. A few minutes of the right kind of cleaning keeps
          both in check, and the wrong kind — a spray bottle aimed at the console, or a polish on
          the belt — can do more harm than the dirt it removes.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">Unplug before cleaning.</strong> Switch off and unplug the
          treadmill at the wall before any cleaning beyond a quick after-session wipe — not just
          the safety key, the plug. Never wipe the belt while it is moving, because the rollers
          are pinch points. Never spray liquid onto the console, into vents or toward the motor
          compartment at the front of the machine, and check your manual for the cleaners your
          manufacturer approves; where it differs from the general advice here, the manual wins.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">How to Clean a Treadmill: The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#why-clean">Why Cleaning a Treadmill Matters</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-you-need">What You Need</a></li>
            <li><a href="#after-every-session">After Every Session: The One-Minute Wipe</a></li>
            <li><a href="#belt-top-surface">How to Clean a Treadmill Belt (Top Surface)</a></li>
            <li><a href="#console-and-screens">Cleaning the Console and Screens</a></li>
            <li><a href="#handrails-and-frame">Handrails, Grips and the Frame</a></li>
            <li><a href="#floor-beneath">The Floor Underneath and Around It</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-to-disinfect">How to Disinfect a Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#cleaners-to-avoid">Cleaners to Avoid on a Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#schedule">A Simple Cleaning Schedule</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-cleaning-reveals">What to Look for While You Clean</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Clean a Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>After each session</strong>, wipe the handrails, console, belt top surface and
            the deck edges with a dry or barely damp microfibre cloth. <strong>Every week or
            two</strong>, unplug the machine and clean properly: the belt's top surface with a
            cloth dampened in mild soapy water, then a clean damp cloth, then a dry one; the
            console and screens with a cloth only lightly dampened, never sprayed directly; the
            rails and frame with the same soap-and-water method; and vacuum the floor around and
            under the machine. Let everything dry before plugging back in.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Avoid</strong> bleach, ammonia, solvents, abrasive pads and scouring powders,
            and anything that leaves a slippery or waxy film — furniture polish, silicone spray or
            oily wipes — on the belt or the side rails you step on. To disinfect, use a product
            your manual permits, applied to a cloth rather than sprayed, and respect the contact
            time on its label.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two jobs that sound like cleaning are separate procedures with their own guides: <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cleaning under the treadmill belt</a>,
            which means slackening the belt and working on the deck, and <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vacuuming the motor compartment</a>,
            which means taking the hood off. This page covers everything else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-clean" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why Cleaning a Treadmill Matters</h2>
<p>
            There are two separate reasons, and they apply to different parts of the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sweat on the surfaces you touch.</strong> Sweat is salty and slightly acidic.
            Left on handrails, console fittings and fasteners, it can dull finishes, pit metal over
            time and work its way into seams. On heart-rate grips it builds a film that does not
            help the readings. On the belt's top surface it mixes with dust into grime that is
            unpleasant at best and, as it builds, can make the surface less consistent underfoot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Dust everywhere else.</strong> A treadmill draws air through the motor
            compartment to cool the motor and controller, and it pulls in whatever is near the
            floor — dust, hair, pet fur and especially carpet fibre. Some of that ends up under the
            belt, where it raises friction, and some ends up in the motor compartment, where it
            reduces cooling. Friction and heat are what wear out motors and controller boards, which
            is why our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance checklist</a> starts with a one-minute wipe after every session.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this needs special products. It needs a routine, a couple of microfibre
            cloths, and the discipline to keep liquid away from the electronics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-you-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What You Need</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Two or three microfibre cloths.</strong> One for damp work, one for drying, and
            a separate soft one for screens. Microfibre lifts grime without needing much liquid,
            which is the whole point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A bowl of warm water with a few drops of mild dish soap.</strong> Mild means a
            gentle washing-up liquid, not a degreaser or a heavy-duty cleaner.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A vacuum with a crevice tool and a soft brush attachment</strong>, for the floor
            around the machine, the deck edges and the outside of the motor hood vents.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A cleaner approved for your screen</strong>, if your treadmill has a touchscreen
            and the manual names one. Otherwise, a cloth barely dampened with water is the safe
            default.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A disinfectant your manual allows</strong>, if you want to disinfect. More on
            choosing one below.
          </p>
          <p>
            What you do not need is a spray bottle of anything aimed at the machine. If a product
            comes in a spray, spray it onto the cloth, away from the treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-every-session" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>After Every Session: The One-Minute Wipe</h2>
<p>
            This is the highest-value cleaning you will do, because it stops sweat drying on and
            dust building up between deeper cleans.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Handrails and heart-rate grips first</strong>, since that is where your hands
            have been. A dry or barely damp microfibre cloth is enough.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The console</strong>, wiping off drips and fingerprints with a dry cloth.
            Sweat that drips onto a console is the main reason it needs cleaning at all, so a towel
            over your shoulder and not over the console helps.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt's top surface</strong>, wherever you can see sweat drips, with the belt
            stopped. You do not need to move the belt for a quick wipe.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The deck edges and side rails</strong> either side of the belt. Dust and fibre
            collect here before being dragged in under the belt, and ten seconds with a cloth
            removes it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then take the safety key out and put it away from the machine, which is not cleaning
            but costs nothing at the same moment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-top-surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Clean a Treadmill Belt (Top Surface)</h2>
<p>
            The running surface is tough, but it is still the surface you trust not to slip, so the
            method is designed to leave it clean, dry and residue-free.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>1. Unplug the treadmill.</strong> Not just the key. You will be moving the belt
            by hand with your fingers near the rollers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. Vacuum or brush off loose debris</strong> from the belt and the deck edges
            first, so you are not grinding grit into the surface with a wet cloth.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. Wipe the visible section</strong> with a microfibre cloth dampened — wrung out
            until it is barely wet — in warm water with a few drops of mild dish soap. Work along the
            grain of the belt surface if it has one. Do not pour or spray water onto the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>4. Advance the belt by hand</strong> to bring the next section into view, pushing
            on the top surface away from the rollers, and repeat until you have been all the way
            round. A chalk mark or a small piece of tape on the edge tells you when you are back where
            you started. Keep your fingers well away from the gap at each end where the belt meets
            the rollers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>5. Go round again with a cloth dampened in clean water</strong>, to remove soap
            residue. Soap left on a belt can make it slick when you next sweat on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>6. Dry it with a clean cloth</strong>, then leave it to air-dry fully before
            plugging back in and using it. Give it at least a short while; it should be completely
            dry to the touch.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep water to the top surface. You are not cleaning under the belt here, and you do not
            want water running off the edges onto the deck, into the rollers or toward the front of
            the machine. If the belt's underside or the deck needs attention, that is <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">a separate job</a> with the belt slackened.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-and-screens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cleaning the Console and Screens</h2>
<p>
            The console is where cleaning most often turns into a repair bill, because liquid that
            reaches the electronics behind a membrane keypad or touchscreen can stop it working.
            The rule is simple: <strong>never spray anything directly onto the console.</strong> Spray or pour onto the cloth, away from the machine, and use a cloth that is damp rather
            than wet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Membrane keypads and plastic panels</strong> clean up well with a barely damp
            microfibre cloth, a touch of mild soap if they are grimy, and a dry cloth afterwards.
            Do not let liquid sit at the edges of buttons or seams, where it can wick behind the
            panel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Touchscreens</strong> usually have coatings that harsh chemicals can damage.
            Turn the screen off or unplug the machine so you can see smears and do not press
            anything by accident. Use a soft, clean microfibre cloth, dry or very lightly dampened
            with water, or the screen cleaner your manual specifies. Avoid paper towels, which can
            scratch, and avoid ammonia-based glass cleaners and other strong products unless your
            manual specifically allows them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speaker grilles, ports and fan vents</strong> on the console should be brushed
            or vacuumed gently, never wiped with a wet cloth.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the console has taken a soaking — a spilled bottle, say — unplug the treadmill and
            leave it unplugged until it has dried thoroughly, and contact the manufacturer if it
            behaves oddly afterwards.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="handrails-and-frame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Handrails, Grips and the Frame</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Handrails.</strong> These collect the most sweat. Wipe with a damp microfibre
            cloth and mild soap, follow with a clean damp cloth, and dry them. On foam-covered
            rails, use the cloth slightly drier and do not soak the foam.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heart-rate grips.</strong> The metal contacts work best when clean, and a film of
            dried sweat can make readings less reliable. Clean them the same way and dry them well.
            Avoid abrasive pads, which can scratch the contact surface.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame and uprights.</strong> Wipe down painted and plastic surfaces with the
            same soapy cloth, then dry. Pay attention to fasteners and joints near where you sweat,
            because that is where corrosion starts. Do not apply oil or polish to the frame; it
            collects dust and can end up on your hands and then the rails.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor hood.</strong> Wipe the outside with a damp cloth and vacuum the
            outside of any vents with the brush attachment. Do not push liquid or the vacuum
            nozzle into the vents. The inside of the hood is the <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor compartment clean</a>,
            done unplugged with the hood off and no liquids at all, typically once a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Folding mechanisms and incline parts.</strong> Wipe away dust, but do not
            lubricate anything unless the manual tells you to and names the product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="floor-beneath" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Floor Underneath and Around It</h2>
<p>
            This is the part most people skip, and it matters more than it looks, because the floor
            under the front of the machine is where the motor's cooling air comes from.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vacuum around the machine every week or so</strong>, and under it whenever you
            can reach. Hair, fluff and carpet fibre near the motor compartment get drawn in, and
            under the belt they become abrasive.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Folding treadmills</strong> make this easy: unplug, <a href="/how-to-fold-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>fold the deck</a> up following the
            manual's procedure, make sure the latch is engaged, and vacuum the floor beneath. <strong>Non-folding treadmills</strong> are heavy and awkward, so reach underneath with a
            crevice tool rather than moving the machine for every clean. If you do need to move it,
            our guide to <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving a treadmill</a> covers how to do it without injury or damage.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A treadmill mat</strong> makes the floor much easier to keep clean, stops carpet
            fibre being drawn into the machine, and protects the floor from the odd drip of
            lubricant. Our guide to whether <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">you need a treadmill mat</a> covers the trade-offs. Lift the mat and clean under it occasionally too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-disinfect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Disinfect a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Most home treadmills do not need disinfecting after every session. Cleaning removes the
            grime that matters for the machine. Disinfecting makes sense when someone in the house
            has been ill, when a machine is shared by several people, or when you have bought a <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>used treadmill</a> and want a fresh start.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the manual first.</strong> Some manufacturers list cleaners and
            disinfectants that are safe for their surfaces, and some specifically warn against
            alcohol, bleach or particular chemicals on the console, the screen or the belt. If the
            manual is silent, contact the manufacturer or stick to mild soap and water.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Focus on touch points.</strong> Handrails, heart-rate grips, the console buttons
            and the safety key and clip are where hands go. The belt's top surface is touched by
            shoes, and cleaning it with soap and water is usually sufficient.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Apply to a cloth, not the machine.</strong> Use a disinfectant wipe, or spray a
            permitted product onto a cloth away from the treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Respect the contact time.</strong> Disinfectants only work if the surface stays
            wet for the time stated on the label, which is often several minutes. Then wipe with a
            clean damp cloth to remove residue, especially on grips and anything that will be under
            your hands, and dry.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep it off the electronics.</strong> Screens and keypads get the gentlest
            treatment — the approved product on a barely damp cloth, or plain water if nothing is
            approved.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaners-to-avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cleaners to Avoid on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Bleach and ammonia.</strong> Harsh on plastics, rubber, coatings and screens,
            and bleach residue on a belt or grips is not something you want against skin.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Solvents and degreasers</strong>, including acetone, paint thinners and strong
            kitchen degreasers. They can attack plastics, the belt's surface and the adhesives that
            hold the belt's layers together.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Abrasive pads and scouring powders.</strong> They scratch consoles and grips and
            can roughen the belt surface.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anything that leaves a film on the belt or foot rails.</strong> Furniture polish,
            silicone spray, oily wipes, waxes and "protectant" sprays make a running surface
            slippery, which is a fall risk at any speed. Silicone belongs under the belt as a
            lubricant, applied as the manual says, never on top. Our page on whether you can <a href="/can-you-use-wd40-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">use WD-40 on a treadmill</a> explains why aerosols are a particular problem near the deck and motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anything sprayed toward the motor compartment.</strong> The motor, controller
            board and mains wiring sit under the hood at the front, and sprays drift.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Steam cleaners and hoses.</strong> Water and heat into a machine full of
            electronics and bearings is never a good idea.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Simple Cleaning Schedule</h2>
<p>
            Cleaning fits around the maintenance schedule rather than replacing any of it. As a
            general pattern for a home machine:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>After every session:</strong> dry or barely damp wipe of handrails, grips,
            console, belt drips and deck edges.</li>
            <li><strong>Weekly or fortnightly:</strong> unplugged clean of the belt top surface,
            console, rails and frame; vacuum around and under the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Every 40 to 50 hours of use:</strong> check and lubricate the deck as your
            manual specifies, which includes wiping the deck and belt underside first. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a> covers it.</li>
            <li><strong>Annually, or every six months on carpet or with pets:</strong> clean fully
            under the belt and vacuum the motor compartment.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Use frequency, people and environment to adjust. Two daily users, a garage, a shedding
            dog or deep carpet all justify doing the dust-related jobs more often. Our full <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance guide</a> puts all of it in one place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-cleaning-reveals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Look for While You Clean</h2>
<p>
            Cleaning is the one time you look closely at the whole machine, so it is worth using
            the opportunity.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt edges.</strong> Fraying, curling or separation between the layers means the
            belt is wearing, and a belt that is creeping to one side may need its tracking adjusted
            — our guide to <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring a treadmill belt</a> covers how.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Black dust.</strong> Fine black powder around the rollers or at the front of the
            deck is often belt or drive <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt wear</a>, and is worth investigating.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Loose fasteners and cracked plastic</strong> on the rails, uprights and hood.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Damaged power cord or plug.</strong> Any cuts, crushing or scorching mean stop
            and replace it before use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Smells.</strong> A machine that smells of sweat needs a clean and some air. A
            machine that smells of hot rubber or electrics needs our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-burning-smell/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill burning smell</a>,
            not air freshener.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes When Cleaning a Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Each of these is well-intentioned, and each turns a cleaning job into a repair or a safety problem.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Spraying cleaner directly onto the console</h3>
              <p>Liquid wicks behind membrane keypads and screen bezels and can reach the electronics. Spray onto a cloth away from the machine, use it damp rather than wet, and dry afterwards.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaving a film on the belt</h3>
              <p>Polish, silicone spray, oily wipes and soap residue all make the running surface slippery. Rinse with a clean damp cloth after soap, dry the belt, and never apply any product to the top surface.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Cleaning the belt while it is running</h3>
              <p>Holding a cloth against a moving belt puts your hand next to the rollers, which are pinch points. Unplug the machine and advance the belt by hand instead.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the floor</h3>
              <p>The motor draws its cooling air from floor level, and whatever is there — dust, hair, carpet fibre — ends up in the motor compartment and under the belt. Vacuuming around the machine is part of cleaning it.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Damp, mild and dry.</strong> A barely damp microfibre cloth,
            mild soap and a dry finish clean every surface you touch. Unplug first, advance the belt
            by hand rather than running it, and never spray liquid onto the console or toward the
            motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep films off the belt, use only disinfectants your manual allows, and vacuum the
            floor around the machine. For the deck and the motor compartment, follow the dedicated
            guides to <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">cleaning under the belt</a> and the motor hood.
          </p>
        </div>

